\section{Probabilistic Programming in Depth}\label{sec:prob-depth}

Probabilistic programming languages extend the programming paradigm with the ability to express and reason about uncertainty.

\subsection{The Probabilistic Programming Model}\label{sec:prob-model}

\begin{definition}[Probabilistic Program Semantics]\label{def:prob-semantics}
A probabilistic program defines a \emph{probability measure} on its output space. The semantics of a probabilistic program $P$ is:
\[
  \llbracket P \rrbracket = \mu_P \in \mathcal{M}(V)
\]
where $\mathcal{M}(V)$ is the space of measures on the output value space $V$, and $\mu_P$ is the measure induced by the program's random choices.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Inference Algorithms}\label{sec:inference-algorithms}

The programming system must provide inference algorithms to compute posterior distributions:

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Inference algorithms in probabilistic programming}
\label{tab:inference-algorithms}
\begin{tabular}{lp{4cm}p{4cm}}
\hline
\textbf{Algorithm} & \textbf{Description} & \textbf{Used By} \\
\hline
MCMC (HMC, NUTS) & Markov chain Monte Carlo sampling & Stan, Pyro \\
Variational inference & Optimize a parametric approximation & Pyro, Edward \\
Sequential Monte Carlo & Particle filtering for sequential models & WebPPL \\
Exact inference & Enumeration for discrete models & PSI \\
Compiling to inference & Transform program to inference code & Hansei, FunSPN \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

% Annotated walkthrough
\subsection{Building an Exact Oracle for Weighted Discrete Traces}

A probabilistic compiler might change execution order or share portions
of a computation between equivalent traces. To check such changes, a
small reference interpreter can enumerate all traces and aggregate
their weights by output. It should compare distributions rather than
the order in which either implementation encountered the traces.
A finite model is especially useful when designing semantics for
observation, where dropping one likelihood factor can change the
answer without changing the set of possible outputs.

\textbf{Contract.} Given the complete finite trace list for a
terminating discrete model, each trace supplies an output and an
exact nonnegative mass: the product of its sample probabilities
and likelihood factors. Report the normalized output distribution
and total mass, or an undefined conditional if the mass is zero.
Distinct list entries represent distinct executions, even if they
return the same output.

\begin{sudocode}[Aggregate before normalizing]
\Let{H}{\text{map with default mass zero}}
\Let{Z}{0}
\For{each completed trace $(v,w)$}
  \To{Equal outputs contribute to one probability bucket.}
  \Let{H[v]}{H[v]+w}
  \Let{Z}{Z+w}
\EndFor
\If{$Z=0$}
  \State \Return undefined conditional
\EndIf
\To{Normalize by surviving mass, not by the number of traces.}
\For{each key $v$ of $H$}
  \Let{H[v]}{H[v]/Z}
\EndFor
\State \Return $(H,Z)$
\end{sudocode}

\textbf{Trace.} Draw uniformly from \(\{1,2,3,4\}\);
observe that the draw is greater than \(1\), and return its
parity. The traces for \(2,3,4\) have weight \(1/4\) apiece.
Even accumulates \(1/2\), odd \(1/4\), and \(Z=3/4\).
The reported distribution is even \(2/3\), odd \(1/3\).
Changing the observation to ``greater than \(4\)'' returns
undefined conditional, not a uniform distribution.

After any processed prefix, \(H[v]\) is exactly that prefix's
mass returning \(v\), and \(Z\) sums all buckets. The calculation
is linear in enumerated traces plus output buckets, counting
rational operations rather than their bit cost. Enumeration can
still grow exponentially in the number of random choices, which
is why this is an oracle for small fixtures, not an inference
strategy for arbitrary models.

\subsection{Probabilistic Programming and Language Design}\label{sec:prob-lang-design}

Probabilistic programming raises interesting language design questions:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Measure representation}: how should probability distributions be represented? As density functions, samples, or symbolic expressions?
  \item \textbf{Conditioning semantics}: what does it mean to condition on an observation in a program with loops and recursion?
  \item \textbf{Inference modularity}: can inference be decomposed compositionally, or must it consider the entire program?
  \item \textbf{Efficiency}: how can the compiler optimize probabilistic programs for efficient inference?
\end{itemize}

